  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Late last week, the official consumer price index \ninflation figure rose to 4.2% year-over-year, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "highest annualized rate seen since the postcoid \nprice surge of 2022. This by itself was not a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "great result. Even though these official numbers \nreally just confirmed what most people already  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16400,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knew, things are getting more expensive. But what \nthey didn't capture is what is likely to still be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coming down the pipe. Last month, the producer \nprice index, which tracks a basket of items that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "businesses use to provide goods and services to \nconsumers, saw its largest single increase in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "last decade, with the only exception of a single \nmonth at the height of post-pandemic inflation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, the reasons are pretty obvious. Fuel prices \ntrickle through to almost everything that needs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be transported. Sprinkle in persistent \ntariff uncertainty, market consolidation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46000,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a lot of resources being redirected towards \ndata center buildouts instead of providing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "service infrastructure to regular people and \nbusinesses. Now, eventually these companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will either be forced to accept lower profit or \njust pass these higher prices along to consumers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which will ultimately be a decision made for them \nby how much more those consumers can financially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bear. Again, not exactly surprising stuff anymore. \nAnd if anything, the cost of living crisis we are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "currently experiencing is arguably not as bad as \nwider economic conditions might suggest, for now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least. But the implications of these numbers \nare worth understanding because they are likely to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean a lot more than just yet even higher prices. \nThis kind of inflation is putting a new Fed in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very difficult position. The logical move would \nbe to raise interest rates. But that runs the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "risks of sweeping out the last leg holding up the \neconomy, which is the stock market. A stock market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is already gearing up to digest three mega \nIPOs in the next few months. The other options  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "involve either sacrificing the American consumer, \nthe American worker, or the American dollar. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other unfortunate reality worth keeping in mind \nis that no matter what we do with our financial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levers, we can't print more oil. And any price \nincreases from here are going to be on top of a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cost of living level that was already forcing a \nlot of people to go without. Are you concerned,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mr. President, about the latest inflation number \nwhich came out this morning? Could that be a No,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I love it. The numbers were right. You know what \nI really love? I love the inflation. spending all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that money on housing, food, child care, \ntransportation, health, and taxes. There's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing left over. And it's hard to imagine that \nif I'm making $120,000, I'm barely making it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142320,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The numbers at the gas pumps keep going up. 539, \n559, and 579. This is ridiculous. We went from 3  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to 5. Domestically, we've got about 216 million \nbarrels of gasoline available to us. That seems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155920,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like a lot, but 190 million barrels is considered \ncritical. Okay, so inflation has clearly been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162960,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creeping up, and that's on top of levels well \nabove what would be considered optimal. But for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now, at least, there are, or at least were, a \nfew things that have been shielding us from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "full effects of just how unaffordable things have \nbecome. The first of those sacrificial shields is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we have been burning through inventories. In \nearly 2025, companies stocked up on anything and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything they could keep in their warehouses. \nmostly in an attempt to get ahead of tariff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187040,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disruptions. And where possible, they have just \nbeen using their stock piles to keep prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191360,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "competitive for as long as they can. In the first \nquarter of 2025 alone, businesses added around 172  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion worth of inventory, which according to \ngovernment GDP data was the largest quarterly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inventory buildup outside of the pandemic \nrecovery in records going back to the 1940s.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This obviously doesn't apply to perishables that \ncan't be stored indefinitely, but specifically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things like component parts and other goods were \npiled up in bulk. Small businesses didn't have a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "warehouse to do this. So mostly this has been a \nbenefit to larger retailers and B2B suppliers. A  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big box retailer could fill distribution centers \nwith a year's worth of patio furniture before the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tariffs landed. The local warehouse buying through \na distributor got a few extra weeks stock at best,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which was kind of part of the plan. For big \ncompanies, this was an opportunity to run  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anti-competitive pricing without actually running \nanti-competitive pricing. Because in theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243520,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it wasn't their fault that their smaller market \nrivals didn't order supplies before a 30% tax was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slapped on everything. Obviously, this isn't \ngreat, but for consumers and end producers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it at the very least delayed price increases. \nWhen the tariffs hit, Goldman Sachs estimated that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies were only passing along about a fifth \nof the cost increases to consumers. A year later,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their estimates put that figure closer to 3/4 \nand still rising. Researchers at the Fed went  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a step further last month and described what we \nare seeing now as uh full pass through. So, that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cushion is mostly spent. We still don't 100% know \nwhat is happening with tariffs either. The Supreme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Court struck down the biggest batch of them in \nFebruary, but the White House has still been going  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back and forth on new ways to get around these \nrulings and re-implement something in its place.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now, since the first round of tariff rules \nweren't legally enforceable, the government will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at some point have to pay back around $166 billion \nin refunds to about 330,000 importers. Money that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is unlikely to find its way back to consumers. \nThis refund process has been incredibly slow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and administratively difficult, which is again \ndisproportionately impacting smaller businesses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that don't have the time or the manpower to wait \nfor this cash. There was also a lot of speculation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around these rulings with certain companies buying \nthe rights to tariff refunds before they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322240,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "confirmed. They would go to businesses and pay \nthem some money to write up a contract that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entitle them to tariff refunds if and when they \nwere ever handed back. One trade attorney at Foley  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Lardner reported getting hundreds of emails a \nday offering to buy his clients refund claims with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "offers that started around 20 cents on the dollar. \nSo yeah, apparently this is a whole industry now.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A lot of desperate small businesses took those \npretty crappy deals just to get some money back to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make payroll. The investors themselves have made \n400% returns off this desperation within the span  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of just a few months, which is clearly how the \nfree market ought to be working. Now, arguably,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358400,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these investment firms still accepted the risks \nthat these tariffs may never be paid back,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362720,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which case they would lose all the money \nthey paid to these businesses. But it probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn't help that the actual architect of the \ntariff plan in the first place also had direct  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest in an investment firm betting against \nthe legality of those very same tariffs. Now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even outside of particularly egregious examples \nlike that, this financialization of government  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380000,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tools is making it particularly difficult for \nsmaller businesses to compete. But the point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "here is that once inventories run dry, we will \nbe more directly exposed to the increased prices  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to actually make or import stuff, which at the \nmoment is climbing about one and a half times as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fast as the headline inflation number. Now, the \nreason why this pressure on small businesses is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such a big deal is because larger companies \naren't going to cover this gap forever. They  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have accepted tighter margins for now because they \nrealize that there is only so much consumers can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bear. But if smaller businesses are pushed out \nof these markets, there won't be much competition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left stopping major retailers from passing along \nthese input price shocks and then some. So yeah,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this inventory draw down has been softening the \ninflationary blow for the last 12 months. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's the first layer of armor that has more or \nless been used up. But within the last 100 days  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in particular, there has been one inventory cash \nthat we have been drawing on harder than ever.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that's our strategic petroleum reserve. So, \nit's time to learn how money works to find out how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much longer we can hide away the real inflation \nproblem. This video is sponsored by ODO. ODU is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the all-in-one business management platform that \npulls sales, accounting, inventory, manufacturing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "e-commerce, and CRM into a single connected \nsystem. Instead of forcing you to juggle five  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different tools that do not talk to each other, \nwhat makes ODU work is how seamlessly it ties  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything together. You only install the apps you \nactually need and they share data automatically.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We use ODU ourselves. Our How many works library \nruns entirely on ODO and having everything in one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dashboard has made the day-to-day so much \nsmoother. It's also intuitive and highly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customizable, which makes it a good fit for small \nto medium-sized businesses that have limited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technical expertise and want software that adapts \nto how they work instead of the other way around.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And because ODU automates the routine stuff, \ninvoicing, restock alerts, follow-ups, you spend  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "less time doing admin or stitching tools together \nand more time actually running your business.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you want to see how ODU can streamline your \nown business, you can book a free meeting with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an ODU expert or try the full platform free for \n15 days with no credit card required or even keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any single app free for life. Check it out using \nmy link in the description. Okay, so companies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "private stockpiles have quietly been absorbing \na lot of the price pressure for the last year,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the one doing the heaviest lifting right now \nbelongs to the federal government. The strategic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "petroleum reserve is quite literally a huge pool \nof oil kept in four separate underground caves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is in theory made for times like this. \nIf we need a stabilized supply and by extension  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prices during unstable times, we can draw down \nthese reserves to put more oil into the market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and avoid huge swings. It's pretty simple, but it \nwon't last forever. According to the Department  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Energy's own numbers, the reserve held about \n411 million barrels at the start of the year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As of last week, it was down to around 349 \nmillion, sitting at under just half of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
