    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the number has beaten consensus. Stocks and bonds \nboth sold off because traders immediately started  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pricing in rate hikes. In a normal economy, lots \nof people getting new jobs should be a good sign  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for businesses like the ones listed on the stock \nmarket, right? More people with jobs means money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to spend, as well as being an indication \nthat companies are confident enough to hire.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we aren't a normal market at the moment. \nValuations are stretched so far that it would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "take decades of good consumer spending to \npay back a lot of the values we are seeing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is such a long time horizon that it has \nbasically become irrelevant. So what is actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happening is that good jobs numbers remove the one \nexcuse that the Fed could hold up to lower rates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or at least leave them alone. The Fed has a triple \nmandate to maintain stable prices, stable rates,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and maximum employment. If employment is \napparently really good and prices are going up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then in theory they have no choice but to raise \ninterest rates. The brand new Fed chair, Kevin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Walsh, who was only sworn in a few weeks ago, \ncompleted his first ever policy meeting in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1107840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big chair last week with exactly that combination \nsitting on the table. Now, we have already made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another video on why those job numbers may not \nactually be all they really seem in reality. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1117920,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I will leave a link to that below instead of \ngoing over it all again. But the point is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as the job numbers look good, at least \non paper, and the inflation numbers look bad,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interest rates are likely to increase. But okay, \nthis is about inflation, not the stock market. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1132720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the sake of this video, who cares? Current \nstocks are overwhelmingly owned by wealthy people  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who can theoretically afford the loss. According \nto the Fed's own distributional accounts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1142560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wealthiest 10% of households hold around 87% \nof household-owned corporate equities, and the top  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1% alone holds about half. However, those wealthy \npeople are the ones currently doing a large share  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the spending. By Moody's estimates, the top \n10% of earners now account for almost half of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all consumer spending, which is the highest share \nin records going back to 1989. A booming stock  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164640,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market makes these people feel good. And when they \nsee big numbers in their Schwab account, they are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more likely to splurge on goods and services that \nprovide opportunities to every other household.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if Green Line does not go up, that could \nknock out the last leg that has been holding up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our economy for a long time now. Both companies \nand wealthy households would stop spending on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1183760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "capex and luxuries, respectively, eliminating the \nbiggest growth sectors of the economy, potentially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causing an employment crisis just as bad, if not \nworse, than the cost of living crisis. Okay, so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1194480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what if we just let inflation run hot? Well, that \nhas its problems, too, even beyond just higher  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1199600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prices. The US dollar is already under stress \nas the global reserve currency. Something that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1204160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buys us extraordinary privilege in international \nmarkets both for imports and for debt. The dollar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1209520,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "share of global foreign exchange reserves \nhas slipped to its lowest level in roughly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1213280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three decades of IMF records. And the dollar \nindex briefly broke below 97 earlier this year,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to lows it hadn't seen in years before the \nwar sent everything scrambling back into dollars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1224400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If our dollar inflates away, we will start to \nlose that privilege. Now, a cheaper dollar does  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1229360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make American exports more competitive, which is \nwhy some people in Washington have openly argued  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1234000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for one, but that math works a lot better when \none of our biggest imports isn't suddenly around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1238720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "40% more expensive than it was a year ago. Also, \nwe are still a massive net importer of goods and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1244080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "services that will get relatively more expensive \nif our dollar gets weaker, compounding the problem  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1248480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of prices on shelves. In the short term, if wages \nare adjusted upwards, it may help smooth things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1253920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over for a little while. But realistically, it \nwill just leave wage earners further behind on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1258640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "already significant cost of living pressures. \nLong-term, it would significantly undermine the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1263760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easy capital we have been blessed with over the \nlast 80 years. Our debt has always been a burden  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1268880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to carry, and keeping it manageable relies on \nbeing able to grow faster than the debt does,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1273520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while simultaneously doing everything we can to \ncarry the weight responsibly. Right now, that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1278480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "debt is just shy of $40 trillion, and it grew by \nabout $3 trillion over the past year. The economy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1284160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underneath it managed less than two. The interest \nalone is costing around a trillion a year. At the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1290080,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same time, we are doing a lot of things that are \nshifting that weight around while simultaneously  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1293840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shooting ourselves in both feet. Now, one ray of \nhope in these numbers is that out of everything,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1298720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cars are getting cheaper. New vehicle prices \nfell again in this report as manufacturers have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1303520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "undermined their own market in some of the dumbest \nways possible. So, go and watch this video next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1307760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to find out how. And don't forget to like and \nsubscribe to keep on learning how money works."
